      "id": "GENUS_SCOPUS",
      "name": "Scopus",
      "rank": "GENUS",
      "description": "A monotypic genus of medium-sized wading bird instantly recognisable by its hammer-shaped head and backward-pointing crest. The Hamerkop is known for building one of the largest and most elaborate nests of any bird — a massive domed stick structure up to two metres across that can support the weight of a person. It is considered a magical or ominous bird in many African cultures.",
      "lineage": "Hamerkop",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "SCOPUS_UMBRETTA",
          "name": "Scopus umbretta",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "Hamerkop",
          "lineage": "Hamerkop",
          "continents": [
            "Africa"
          ],
          "subspeciesCount": 2,
          "description": "The hamerkop (Scopus umbretta), also called the umbrette, is a medium-sized bird. It is the only living species in the genus Scopus and the family Scopidae. The species and family was long thought to sit with the Ciconiiformes but is now placed with the Pelecaniformes, and its closest relatives are thought to be the pelicans and the shoebill. The shape of its head with a long bill and crest at the back is reminiscent of a hammer, which has given this species its name after the Afrikaans word for hammerhead. It is a medium-sized waterbird with brown plumage. It is found in mainland Africa, Madagascar and Arabia, living in a wide variety of wetlands, including estuaries, lakesides, fish ponds, riverbanks, and rocky coasts. The hamerkop is a sedentary bird that often shows local movements.\nThe hamerkop takes a wide range of prey, mostly fish and amphibians, but shrimps, insects and rodents are taken too. Prey is usually hunted in shallow water, either by sight or touch, but the species is adaptable and will take any prey it can. The species is renowned for its enormous nests, several of which are built during the breeding season. Unusually for a wading bird, the nest has an internal nesting chamber where the eggs are laid. Both parents incubate the eggs and raise the chicks.",
